INSERT INTO public.issue_activity (id, issue_id, kind, actor_name, body, action, created_at, actor_user_id, initiative_id, reply_to_id, payload, edited_at) VALUES (1191, 30, 'activity', 'owner', NULL, 'title', '2026-10-19 02:31:04.682369+00', 'DFppJIU20ig1j5gFRYQIe541IkO7Itfp', NULL, NULL, '{"to": {"value": "iOS Notification Center: group or clear the delivered stack (threadIdentifier)"}, "from": {"value": "#120 iOS Notification Center: group or clear the delivered stack (threadIdentifier)"}}', NULL);
INSERT INTO public.issue_activity (id, issue_id, kind, actor_name, body, action, created_at, actor_user_id, initiative_id, reply_to_id, payload, edited_at) VALUES (1192, 30, 'activity', 'owner', NULL, 'description', '2026-10-19 02:31:04.682369+00', 'DFppJIU20ig1j5gFRYQIe541IkO7Itfp', NULL, NULL, '{"to": {"value": "OWNER ORDER 2026-10-16 (111 planning session): the owner read the SDK 58 release note (expo-notifications on iOS: notifications can be grouped with threadIdentifier) and ordered a ticket; this SUPERSEDES the programme''s standing \"no threadIdentifier grouping\" ruling taken at past re-pins (SDK58-PROGRAMME.md section 16, scope item 2). CURRENT STATE, verified from code at planning 111: Android already delivers exactly one visible notification at a time - `plugins/replacePreviousNotification.js` writes a presentation delegate that posts every notification under one shared tag `athan-notification`, the newest replacing the one before it (row 3, landed 1.27.198); scheduling identifiers are deterministic and date-scoped on both platforms, so a re-arm replaces in place and can never duplicate (device/notifications.ts:46-52); iOS carries NO delivered-notification handling in app code (no dismissal, no threadIdentifier anywhere in the repo): every fired athan and reminder stacks in the iOS Notification Center under the app until the user clears it. THE ROW decides what iOS should do: grouping under a thread (one stack with a header), clearing or replacing delivered notifications, or both; planning measures what the center holds after a multi-athan day on the XS (the 111 deep-dive loop''s evidence feeds it) and the owner rules the shape. Reminders answer too: they join the athan thread or sit apart\n\nStatus note: 2026-10-17, audited (three passes: plan-conformance conforms, one threading SHOULD fixed - the clear now consumes its own timeout flag so a refused clear can no longer reopen the refresh gate hours later on an unrelated pass; one blind SHOULD fixed - the docblock scoped to what open-time clearing delivers; the background fence pinned on the real reschedule path; two test-hygiene nices applied; the unused-export and threadIdentifier suggestions rejected on recorded ground). The change: one iOS-gated `clearDeliveredNotifications` inside `initializeNotifications`, after the permission grant, before the refresh, outside the scheduling lock, never from the background task (a real-path fence test pins it); red-green coverage with a six-mutant pass at 2.0.933 (merged b89c0d3f), audit fixes at 2.0.979. PROOF: the simulator leg complete (four timestamped mock deliveries 01:57/02:04/02:13/02:14, the lock-screen pair one-entry-before/zero-after with the open logged at 02:11:28); the XS glass leg delivered on glass (the banner read at 06:55:03, app closed since 06:53:43) with the neutral open at 06:55:07 - the phone''s center-zero read is a recorded void (three gesture routes failed; the owner''s eyeball is the glass verdict per the repo''s iOS doctrine), and the owner''s phone is returned to the merged prod build (key verified present, schedule rendering on real data 07:22). KNOWN LIMIT, recorded: overnight deliveries still stack on the lock screen until the first app open - open-time clearing is the ruled mechanism (option C), not a post-time replacement (red-green-mutate green on lane/120 at 2.0.933, six mutants reddened; step 2, the XS proof, next): plan at `ai/plans/120-notification-center/` (planned at `d1ce39d9`, 2.0.924; two cold grill rounds, `PLAN OK`): THE OWNER''S RULING TAKEN (in session, quoted in substance): iOS must behave like Android - one visible notification at a time, the newest replacing the one before it, so a sleeper never wakes to a stack. The previous record follows. NOT DONE; the code ticket and its XS proof follow. (two passes, blind and plan-conformance: sixteen findings adjudicated, sixteen confirmed and fixed, none rejected; no code, no build, no device contact; the plan folder and the evidence died in this merge): the delivered-stack question answers as five options with one recommendation, agent-ruled: A status quo, B group under a thread, C clear the delivered stack when the app opens (recommended), D both, and replace-in-place (rejected outright). MEASURED, device-free: iOS carries no delivered-notification handling at all - the four-API sweep over app code is empty (no dismissal, no presented-set read, no badge) and neither composer writes a badge; the foreground handler puts what fires while the app is open into the banner and the list (`hooks/useNotification.ts:12-20`); expo-notifications 58.1.5 (the version the SDK 58 re-pin this lane''s rebase surfaced) applies `threadIdentifier` on iOS (`NotificationRecords.swift:297,369` into the scheduler''s content, `SchedulerModule.swift:135`; landed 58.0.0, expo/expo#49429) and maps it to Android''s notification group key (`ArgumentsNotificationContentBuilder.java:51`; landed 58.1.1), while the content input exposes no `summaryArgument` (`Notifications.types.d.ts:613`, read side `:450-480`, group header only through a category''s `categorySummaryFormat` at `:754`), so one thread equals the app-level group iOS already gives every app (WWDC18-710) and grouping shortens nothing; the three delivered-set routes touch delivered notifications only (`dismissAllNotificationsAsync` to `removeAllDeliveredNotifications`, `PresentationModule.swift:12-39`). REJECTED: thread grouping as the answer (no shortening, and the one header lever the pinned API does expose, a category''s `categorySummaryFormat`, names the group without removing an entry); replacement by one shared identifier (it would collapse the date-scoped candidate-row arming against the 64 budget); every Android-side lever (a group key changes the frozen one-visible face, and Android''s dismissal call routes to `cancelAll`, `ExpoPresentationDelegate.kt:275-277`). RECOMMENDATION AND BLAST RADIUS: CLEAR the delivered stack when the app opens, iOS-gated - one call in `initializeNotifications` (`shared/notifications.ts:572-603`), before the refresh and outside the scheduling lock, at cold launch and on every foreground return and never from the background task (`device/tasks.ts:23-49` with `stores/notifications.ts:1836-1876`); no identifier change, no channel change, no pending-request change, the 64-budget arming, the reminder-never-outruns rule, the Android delegate and the frozen channels all untouched; reminders need no thread of their own, they clear with everything else and join the athan thread if grouping is ever taken. THE NAMED EXPERIMENT waits on the owner''s unlock (the owner''s recorded configuration already delivers a multi-athan day: five at-time rows plus Magrib''s two reminders, of which row 111 measured the three deliveries of that one evening at 17:38, 18:03 and 18:08): read 1 the center''s structure and the Settings grouping mode through agent-device taps with the screenshot transcribed by an image-capable worker, read 2 the unified log''s delivery lines over a bounded window as corroboration, read 3 `getPresentedNotificationsAsync` from a build carrying the diagnostic call; the four void shapes (a zero-entry center, an ungrouped center, a count below the log''s, a phone that still refuses automation), every read''s tool and the evidence path sit in the plan''s section 7, recoverable with `git log --all --oneline -- ai/plans/120-notification-center/PLAN.md`. The audit''s one recorded exception: the skeleton and READY commits both carry 2.0.909, because the rebase''s conflict resolution consumed the READY bump; the three step commits each bump a patch; the DONE commit''s bump and row text were staged empty when its `git add` hit the just-removed plan folder, and both landed in its follow-up commit instead; no history was rewritten. The row ends with the owner''s ruling; the ruling opens the code ticket. OWNER CONFIRMED 2026-10-17 on the real phone (the diagnostic build carrying the mock seed, during 131''s window): two athan stack while the app is closed, and opening the app clears the lock screen and the Notification Center completely - the one read the DONE record left to the owner''s.\n\nNeeds first: #2026, #10, #16"}}', NULL);
